overhead = bits minus the bits of a one-line citation of f(P) in a theory with the same number of axioms.
K = written occurrences of P; under the naive code the overhead grows by LAM*(K-1) = 4.52*(K-1) bits per
    extra symbol of the motive (a citation writes the motive once, K = 1).
bits(dec) = the decodable variant (nd.Proof.bits_decodable: + number of lines, number of tc premises, the
    variable of each subst and exI line); increase over bits: 4.8% to 7.3% on the non-citation rows.

template sizes |f(P)| (symbols): Ind 16, CVI 18, LNP 19  -> prior cost LAM*|f| = Ind 72, CVI 81, LNP 86 bits

pairwise break-even: per use of a form, the extra bits it costs in each theory
  T_Ind vs T_CVI on Ind/CVI data:  T_Ind wins iff p_CVI/p_Ind < 0.600
  T_Ind vs T_LNP on Ind/LNP data:  T_Ind wins iff p_LNP/p_Ind < 0.757
  T_CVI vs T_LNP on CVI/LNP data:  T_CVI wins iff p_LNP/p_CVI < 1.172
  same three ratios under the decodable code: 0.609, 0.765, 1.180
  (each ratio is for L1-sch with THIS derivation library: the costs are upper bounds, so the ratios are not
  the ratios of shortest derivations; see the naive-code ratios at the end of this file for code dependence)

lower-bound sizes: |CVI(P)| = 18, its antecedent 13 symbols; |Ind(P)| = 16, its antecedent 11 symbols;
  |LNP(P)| = 19, its antecedent 4 symbols

